1. PURPOSE AND APPLICABILITY
This official application form is utilized by matriculated undergraduate, postgraduate, and doctoral students of Demo University to request a General Bonafide Student Certificate (Proof of Study). Administered jointly by Academic Affairs (DEPT-ACAD) and the Registrar's Office (DEPT-ADM), the certificate attests to active enrollment, academic degree program, current semester standing, and residential status for official statutory purposes—including passport applications, visa renewals, government welfare schemes, and public commuter transit concessions.

2. ELIGIBILITY REQUIREMENTS (POL-BONAFIDE)
2.1 Active Enrollment Status: The applicant must be currently enrolled with active matriculation status in UniCampus ERP;
2.2 Fee Arrears Clearance: Zero total tuition fee defaults or outstanding financial hold flags on PayVarsity;
2.3 Disciplinary Standing: Students undergoing active disciplinary suspension, debarment, or inquiries before the Proctorial Board (STU-POL-005) are strictly ineligible for bonafide certification.

3. APPLICATION SUBMISSION PROCEDURE (PROC-BONAFIDE-REQ)
3.1 Digital Submission Channel: Students initiate the request digitally via UniCampus ERP by navigating to: Student Services > Certificates > 'General Bonafide Certificate Request' (STU-FRM-001);
3.2 Required Form Fields: Full Student Name, Roll / Registration Number, Degree Program & School, Current Semester, Date of Birth, Father's / Mother's Name, Permanent Home Address, and Explicit Purpose of Request (e.g., Regional Passport Office, Nationalised Bank, Transit Authority);
3.3 Document Uploads: Where mandated by external authorities (such as passport appointment schedules or visa verification notices), an official requirement letter must be attached as a PDF.

4. PROCESSING TIMELINE AND ISSUANCE SLA
4.1 Standard SLA: General bonafide certificates are generated digitally within two (2) working days following submission;
4.2 Digital Authentication: Certificates are issued as tamper-evident, digitally signed PDF documents bearing an official university seal, Registrar signature facsimile, and a unique verifiable dynamic QR code linked to the public UniCampus verification portal;
4.3 Hard Copy Attestation: Where embossed physical ink stamps are required by foreign embassies or statutory bodies, students present the printed PDF at the Registrar Services Counter (Chanakya Bhavan, Ground Floor Visitor Hub) for embossing.

1. PURPOSE AND INTER-DEPARTMENTAL FUNCTION (REL-003)
This application form enables enrolled students to apply for an official, certified Education Loan Fee Estimation Statement and specialized institutional bonafide letter required by nationalized and commercial banks for loan sanctioning. This document acts as an operational bridge between Academic Affairs (DEPT-ACAD, verifying academic duration and credit residency) and Finance and Accounts (DEPT-FIN, providing certified fee schedules).

2. SCOPE OF ESTIMATED FINANCIAL COMPONENTS
The resulting certified statement encapsulates complete, multi-year projected institutional expenditures across the remaining academic duration:
- Annual Tuition Fees and Specialized Laboratory / Studio Charges;
- Mandatory University Registration, Examination, and Convocation Fees;
- Institutional Caution Deposits (Refundable under FIN-POL-003);
- Standard Residential Hostel Accommodation and Dining Mess Fees (where applicable);
- Standard Book Bank and Academic Resource Surcharges.

3. APPLICATION AND SUBMISSION WORKFLOW
3.1 Submission: The student accesses UniCampus ERP > Student Services > 'Education Loan Fee Estimation Request' (STU-FRM-002);
3.2 Applicant Information: Student enters Lending Bank Name, Branch IFSC Code, Loan Account / File Number, and Residential Status (Day Scholar vs Hostel Resident);
3.3 Supporting Attachments: Student uploads the official Bank Loan Sanction In-Principle Letter or Bank Fee Schedule Proforma.

4. VERIFICATION WORKFLOW AND ISSUANCE SLA
4.1 Multi-Stage Audit: Academic Affairs validates active progression and program duration (e.g., 4 years for B.Tech). Finance and Accounts reconciles fee ledgers and inserts official financial fee tables;
4.2 Processing Timeline: Specialized fee estimation statements require four (4) working days from digital application;
4.3 Issuance Format: Hard copy printed on official university security letterhead, jointly endorsed with wet-ink signatures and embossed seals of the Student Fee Accounts Officer (DEPT-FIN) and University Registrar (DEPT-ADM);
4.4 Collection Point: Dispatched via Registrar Services Counter, Chanakya Bhavan, Ground Floor Visitor Hub.

1. STATUTORY PURPOSE AND REGULATORY GOVERNANCE
This application form regulates the formal institutional discharge and statutory documentation issuance for students who have graduated, completed academic degree programs, or formally withdrawn from Demo University. Governed by POL-TRANSFER-CERT and PROC-TC-MIGRATION, this form initiates the processing and dispatch of the statutory Transfer Certificate (TC) and Inter-University Migration Certificate.

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY DISAMBIGUATION (TC AND LC)
2.1 Transfer Certificate (TC under DEPT-ADM / STU-FRM-003 / ADM-POL-004): The official discharge certificate detailing date of admission, date of leaving, academic conduct, and release from university jurisdiction. Must not be confused with 'Training Coordinator' (TC under DEPT-CPTD / CPTD-STF-001);
2.2 Leaving Certificate (LC under DEPT-ADM / ADM-POL-004): The colloquial and regional statutory synonym for the institutional discharge document. Must not be confused with 'Library Committee' (LC under DEPT-LIB / LIB-GOV-001).

3. MANDATORY PRE-CONDITIONS AND NO-DUES CLEARANCE (ADM-SOP-001)
No application for a Transfer Certificate or Migration Certificate can be processed until 100% completion of the University Institutional No-Dues Clearance workflow on UniCampus ERP:
- Clearance of library book loans and circulation fines (DEPT-LIB);
- Hostel room vacation sign-off and key surrender (DEPT-HSTL);
- Engineering lab hardware component reconciliation (Academic Departments);
- Full settlement of tuition and campus accounts ledgers (DEPT-FIN).

4. APPLICATION PROCEDURE AND DISPATCH TIMELINE
4.1 Online Form Lodging: Following No-Dues clearance, the applicant accesses UniCampus ERP > Student Services > 'TC & Migration Request' (STU-FRM-003);
4.2 Applicant Submissions: Student enters Reason for Departure (Graduation / Admission to Another Institution / Personal Withdrawal), Destination University Name, and Verified Mailing Postal Address;
4.3 Dispatch SLA: Certificates are signed by the Registrar, embossed with the official University Common Seal, and ready for physical collection or registered speed-post dispatch within seven (7) working days;
4.4 Duplicate Certificate Norms: A duplicate TC is never issued routinely; applicants must submit an authenticated police non-traceable certificate and an attested notary affidavit.

1. PURPOSE AND APPLICABILITY
This form regulates the submission, multi-tiered administrative routing, and attendance adjustments for students officially deputed to represent Demo University in inter-collegiate sports, technical hackathons, cultural festivals, and academic conferences. Governed by POL-OD and PROC-OD-SANCTION.

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY DISAMBIGUATION (OD)
Within university documentation, the acronym 'OD' denotes two completely different concepts:
2.1 On-Duty Attendance Exemption (OD under DEPT-DSA / STU-FRM-004 / POL-OD): The official administrative process of excusing class absence for approved university representation;
2.2 Organizational Development (OD under DEPT-MGMT / MGT-CRS-101): The core academic MBA management course module.

3. STATUTORY LIMITS AND SUBMISSION RULES
3.1 Ten-Day Semester Cap: A student is entitled to a maximum cumulative limit of ten (10) working days of On-Duty leave per academic semester across all courses;
3.2 Pre-Event Submission SLA: Applications must be submitted on ResolveDesk (Category: Student Affairs > On-Duty Sanction) at least three (3) working days prior to departure;
3.3 Mandatory Pre-Event Documentation: Official invitation brochure, match fixture/event schedule, and formal faculty endorsement letter;
3.4 Post-Event Verification SLA: Within five (5) working days of returning to campus, the student must upload the verified Certificate of Participation or Merit Certificate;
3.5 Attendance Adjustment: AttendEdge programmatically converts absent session stamps for sanctioned dates into 'Excused Present (OD)' for SEE hall ticket calculation.

1. PURPOSE AND APPLICABILITY
This form is utilized by enrolled students whose aggregate course attendance has fallen strictly within the condonable band (65.0% to 74.9%) due to authenticated severe illness, surgery, contagious quarantine, or hospitalization, seeking statutory attendance condonation to avoid exam detention. Governed by POL-ATTEND, ACAD-POL-003, and PROC-ATTEND-COND.

2. STATUTORY BOUNDARIES AND ABSOLUTE NON-CONDONABILITY
2.1 Condonable Range: Discretionary condonation by the Dean of Academic Affairs applies strictly to attendance between 65.0% and 74.9%;
2.2 Sub-65% Prohibition: Attendance below 65.0% cannot be condoned under any circumstance; students with attendance < 65% are summarily detained ('W'/'SA' grade);
2.3 Submission Deadline: This form and supporting medical proofs must be lodged on ResolveDesk at least five (5) working days prior to the commencement of Semester End Examinations.

3. MANDATORY SUPPORTING DOCUMENTATION
3.1 Clinical Proofs: Detailed hospital discharge summary, physician consultation notes, registered medical practitioner prescriptions, and diagnostic lab reports;
3.2 Health Centre Verification Slip: Prior to lodging this form, the student must report in person to the Campus Health Centre within three (3) days of resuming classes. The Senior Chief Medical Officer examines documentation and issues an authenticated University Medical Verification Slip bearing a unique validation code;
3.3 Endorsement Routing: Faculty Advisor and HOD review course attendance records on AttendEdge and digitally endorse the application;
3.4 Detention Waiving: Dean's digital approval lifts the examination detention flag in UniCampus ERP, unblocking hall ticket downloads on ExamShield.

1. PURPOSE AND STATUTORY SCOPE
This application form enables candidates who appeared in Semester End Examinations (SEE) to formally inspect evaluated digital answer booklets, request arithmetic summation re-totalling, or petition for full blind academic re-evaluation. Governed by POL-REEVAL and PROC-REEVAL-APP.

2. FEE SCHEDULE AND TIMELINES (FIN-POL-002)
2.1 Answer Script Digital Viewing: Application lodged on ExamShield within seven (7) calendar days of result publication; fee of Rs. 400 per course script;
2.2 Arithmetic Re-totalling: Application lodged within seven (7) days; fee of Rs. 300 per paper (processing SLA: 3 working days);
2.3 Formal Academic Re-evaluation: Application lodged within seven (7) days of viewing the answer script; fee of Rs. 1,200 per paper (processing SLA: 15 working days).

3. RE-EVALUATION WORKFLOW AND ARBITRATION NORMS (REL-010)
3.1 Blind Re-assessment: Original evaluator markings and scores are digitally masked; the script is reassessed independently by an external senior examiner;
3.2 15% Deviation Threshold: If the revised mark differs by >= 15% from the original score, the script is automatically referred to a third neutral senior evaluator for binding arbitration;
3.3 Grade Finality: The grade finalized after re-evaluation supersedes the earlier grade, even if the resulting mark is lower;
3.4 50% Fee Refund: If re-evaluation results in an upward mark revision of >= 10%, a 50% refund (Rs. 600) is credited to the student's PayVarsity account.

1. PURPOSE AND APPLICABILITY
This form is utilized by enrolled students to declare the loss, theft, severe physical defacement, or technical chip failure of their official Demo University Smart RFID Identity Card, and request duplicate credential printing and access re-provisioning. Governed by POL-ID-CARD, PROC-ID-REPLACE, and REL-009.

2. REPLACEMENT PROCEDURE AND TIMELINE
2.1 Loss Incident Logging: Student logs into ResolveDesk and submits this form under Category: Security > Lost ID Card. CampusPass immediately revokes the lost RFID chip serial number across all turnstiles;
2.2 Fee Payment: Student remits the prescribed non-refundable replacement fee of Rs. 500 online via PayVarsity (FIN-POL-002);
2.3 Physical Verification: Student presents the PayVarsity fee payment receipt and a recognized government photo identity card at the Central Security Office (Main Entrance Gate Security Complex);
2.4 Re-issuance SLA: The duplicate Smart RFID card is printed, encoded, and handed over within forty-eight (48) hours of fee settlement;
2.5 Automated Re-synchronization: CampusPass propagates the new RFID chip identifier to the Valmiki Central Library (LibNet), hostel biometric speed gates (DormTrack), and campus commuter buses (TransPass).

1. PURPOSE AND INTER-DEPARTMENTAL SCOPE (REL-006)
This comprehensive Standard Operating Procedure establishes the mandatory institutional clearance protocol required of every student leaving Demo University—whether upon successful graduation or formal program withdrawal. Clearance verifies the total settlement of academic, residential, operational, and fiscal obligations across five distinct departments prior to releasing provisional degree certificates, sealed transcripts, Transfer Certificates (TC), and refundable caution deposits.

2. FIVE-DEPARTMENT CLEARANCE SIGN-OFF MATRIX
The digital No-Dues clearance workflow is executed strictly through UniCampus ERP and requires sequential sign-offs across five operational divisions:
2.1 Department 1: Central Library (DEPT-LIB / LIB-POL-001): Verifies the return of all circulating books, inter-library loans, and clearance of overdue fine ledgers on LibNet;
2.2 Department 2: Hostel Administration (DEPT-HSTL / HSTL-REG-001): Conducts room inventory inspection, verifies room key surrender, and audits mess bill ledgers on DormTrack;
2.3 Department 3: Parent Academic Department & Labs (DEPT-CSE, DEPT-ECE, etc.): Department lab in-charge verifies the return of all issued hardware development kits, microcontrollers, and clears accidental breakage slips (REL-018);
2.4 Department 4: Campus Operations & Sports (DEPT-OPS / DEPT-SAC): Confirms return of athletic equipment and lockers at the Student Activity Centre;
2.5 Department 5: Finance and Accounts Office (DEPT-FIN / FIN-POL-003): Audits entire multi-year fee ledger on PayVarsity, resolves outstanding tuition or transport arrears, authorizes the release of the refundable caution deposit, and issues final fiscal clearance.

3. SEQUENTIAL DIGITAL WORKFLOW IN UNICAMPUS ERP
3.1 Step 1 (Initiation): Student logs into UniCampus ERP > Student Services > 'Institutional No-Dues Clearance' during the designated graduation audit window (May-June);
3.2 Step 2 (Automated Verification): ERP queries LibNet and DormTrack database APIs to identify outstanding liabilities;
3.3 Step 3 (Departmental Queue Routing): Clearances route automatically to departmental dashboards for designated officer digital sign-off (SLA: 5 working days);
3.4 Step 4 (Caution Deposit Credit): Finance Office initiates automated electronic fund transfer (NEFT) of the verified caution deposit (FIN-POL-003) to the student's verified bank account within fourteen (14) working days;
3.5 Step 5 (Credential Release): Examination Cell generates official consolidated mark transcripts and provisional degree certificates; Admissions Office dispatches Transfer and Migration certificates.

1. PURPOSE AND INTER-DEPARTMENTAL WORKFLOW (REL-003)
This standard operating procedure establishes the administrative protocols, verification hierarchies, and delivery channels for issuing official Bonafide Certificates and Proof of Study documentation. While students often make brief inquiries ('how to get bonafide'), the required operational workflow depends fundamentally on the intended purpose:
1.1 General Study / Citizenship Proof: Requires active academic enrollment verification by Academic Affairs (DEPT-ACAD) and Registrar signature (DEPT-ADM);
1.2 Bank Education Loan Letters: Requires certified multi-year annual institutional fee breakdowns formulated by the Finance and Accounts Office (DEPT-FIN).

2. CATEGORIES AND SERVICE-LEVEL AGREEMENTS (SLAS)
2.1 Category 1: General Bonafide Study Certificate (SLA: 2 Working Days): Issued for public bus transit concessions, regional passport office appointments, student software licenses, and competitive examination registrations;
2.2 Category 2: Specialized Loan Fee Estimation Letter (SLA: 4 Working Days): Formulated for commercial bank educational loans, detailing institutional tuition, hostel fees, and caution deposits;
2.3 Category 3: Visa / Embassy Sponsorship Attestation (SLA: 4 Working Days): Formulated for international student visa renewals or student travel visas requiring formal university sponsorship statements.

3. APPLICATION PROCEDURE VIA UNICAMPUS ERP
3.1 Digital Request Submission: Students log in to UniCampus ERP, navigate to Student Services > Certificates, select the appropriate certificate type, enter the specific recipient statutory authority, and upload prerequisite requirement letters;
3.2 Automated System Auditing: The ERP engine validates active enrollment, verifies zero active disciplinary suspension flags, and checks for financial blocks on PayVarsity;
3.3 Digital Certificate Generation: Approved general certificates are issued as downloadable PDF files bearing a secure, verifiable university cryptographic QR code;
3.4 Embossed Wet-Ink Attestation: When wet-ink stamps or wax seals are demanded by foreign consulates or banks, students present the printed certificate at Counter 2, Chanakya Bhavan Visitor Hub.

1. PURPOSE AND STATUTORY REACH
This policy defines the official protocols governing voluntary student withdrawal, program terminations, graduation releases, and the issuance of statutory Transfer Certificates (TC) and Inter-University Migration Certificates. Promulgated by the Admissions Office and Registrar Secretariat (DEPT-ADM).

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY AND ENTITY DISAMBIGUATION (TC AND LC)
2.1 Transfer Certificate (TC under DEPT-ADM / ADM-POL-004): The legal educational credential certifying academic release, enrollment duration, moral conduct, and non-liability. Must not be confused with 'Training Coordinator' (TC under DEPT-CPTD / CPTD-STF-001), the administrative staff coordinating corporate aptitude bootcamps;
2.2 Leaving Certificate (LC under DEPT-ADM / ADM-POL-004): The regional statutory synonym for the Transfer Certificate issued upon course completion. Must not be confused with 'Library Committee' (LC under DEPT-LIB / LIB-GOV-001), the advisory body governing book procurement quotas and journal subscriptions.
- Disambiguation Principle: Searches mentioning leaving college, migrating to another university, or duplicate school discharge refer to Transfer Certificate / Leaving Certificate (ADM-POL-004).

3. MANDATORY INSTITUTIONAL CLEARANCE PREREQUISITE (ADM-SOP-001)
Under no circumstances may a Transfer Certificate or Migration Certificate be generated without 100% completion of the University No-Dues Clearance protocol in UniCampus ERP, verifying complete settlement across Library, Hostels, Department Labs, and Accounts.

4. CERTIFICATE ISSUANCE AND DISPATCH TIMELINE (REL-015)
4.1 Processing Timeline: Following successful No-Dues clearance and submission of STU-FRM-003, statutory certificates are prepared within seven (7) working days;
4.2 Official Sealing: Certificates are formally signed by the University Registrar and embossed with the metal common seal;
4.3 Delivery Methods: Graduating students may collect certificates in person at the Registrar Counter (Chanakya Bhavan) upon surrendering their student RFID smart card, or request delivery via registered speed post upon settling postal courier fees on PayVarsity;
4.4 Duplicate Certificate Protocol: If an original TC or Migration Certificate is lost or destroyed, a duplicate can only be issued upon physical submission of an official police non-traceable report, an attested legal notary affidavit, and a duplicate fee of Rs. 1,000 paid via PayVarsity.

1. PURPOSE AND STATUTORY COMPLIANCE (REL-019)
This manual establishes operational protocols, regulatory reporting mandates, and institutional support workflows for international foreign national students enrolled across degree programs at Demo University. Administered by the International Admissions Wing of DEPT-ADM to ensure full compliance with national immigration statutes and Foreigners Regional Registration Office (FRRO) directives.

2. MANDATORY FRRO REGISTRATION (FORM S PROTOCOL)
2.1 14-Day Mandatory Window: Every foreign national student holding a student visa must complete online registration on the government e-FRRO portal within fourteen (14) days of arrival in the country;
2.2 Form S Submission: The International Student Advisor verifies passport credentials and submits digital institutional endorsement ('Form S') through the national immigration portal;
2.3 Address Proof: The student is issued an official certified Residential Bonafide Certificate by Hostel Administration (DEPT-HSTL / DormTrack) validating on-campus dormitory residency.

3. STUDENT VISA EXTENSION WORKFLOW
Students requiring visa renewals or extension endorsements must execute the following protocol at least sixty (60) days prior to visa expiration:
3.1 Application on ERP: The student logs into UniCampus ERP > International Services > 'Visa Extension Recommendation';
3.2 Academic Progression Clearance: Academic Affairs validates active academic progression, current semester attendance >= 75%, and zero pending disciplinary proceedings;
3.3 Formal Recommendation Letter: The International Admissions Office prepares a formal institutional Visa Extension Recommendation Letter signed by the Registrar, detailing degree duration, anticipated graduation year, and financial bona fides;
3.4 Police Liaison: The Campus Security Department (DEPT-SEC) assists local law enforcement and intelligence officers conducting mandatory statutory background checks.

1. STATUTORY MANDATE AND POLICY REACH
Promulgated by the Academic Council, this policy regulates the eligibility criteria, issuance standards, verification protocols, and delivery channels for official Bonafide Student Certificates, Proof of Study letters, and institutional document endorsements at Demo University.

2. STATUTORY ELIGIBILITY CONSTRAINTS
2.1 Active Matriculation: Bonafide certification is issued exclusively to students maintaining active matriculated enrollment in good standing;
2.2 Disciplinary Bar: Students facing active disciplinary suspension, debarment, or pending inquiries before the Proctorial Board (STU-POL-005) cannot be issued bonafide certificates;
2.3 Financial Default Bar: Total default of semester tuition fees blocks the generation of bonafide letters until the financial hold is liquidated in PayVarsity.

3. CLASSIFICATION OF CERTIFICATES AND PROCESSING SLAS
3.1 Standard Bonafide Certificate (SLA: 2 Working Days): Attests to active study for transit passes, passport verification, or student concessions. Issued as a tamper-evident digital PDF with a cryptographic QR code;
3.2 Specialized Financial Bonafide (SLA: 4 Working Days): Detailed fee structure letter formulated for bank education loans, containing certified tuition, hostel, and fee schedules verified by the Finance Office;
3.3 Visa and Embassy Sponsorship Letters (SLA: 4 Working Days): Specialized endorsements for consular authorities.

4. VERIFICATION INFRASTRUCTURE
External statutory bodies (banks, embassies, police) verify issued certificates by scanning the embedded cryptographic QR code, which resolves securely to the university authentication engine.

1. STATUTORY CONSTITUTION AND JURISDICTION
The Executive Council (designated in institutional statutes as the 'EC') is the apex executive and governing authority of Demo University, constituted under the foundational University Charter. The Council oversees institutional management, capital property investments, annual revenue budgets, statutory appointments, and the enactment of university statutes.

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY AND ENTITY DISAMBIGUATION (EC)
Within Demo University systems and communications, the abbreviation 'EC' possesses three completely distinct institutional meanings:
2.1 Executive Council (EC under Governance / GOV-ACT-001): The apex governing board and policy-making tribunal chaired by the Vice-Chancellor;
2.2 Examination Cell (EC under DEPT-EXAM / EXAM-GUI-005): The confidential evaluation and testing administration office headed by the Controller of Examinations;
2.3 Electronics and Communication Engineering (EC under DEPT-ECE / ECE-CRS-001): The academic engineering department and degree branch.
- Disambiguation Rule: Text referencing university-wide fiscal resolutions, statute amendments, or Board of Management directives refers to the Executive Council (GOV-ACT-001).

3. COUNCIL COMPOSITION AND MEMBERSHIP
The Executive Council comprises:
3.1 Chairperson: The Vice-Chancellor (ROLE-VC), presiding officer;
3.2 Secretary: The University Registrar (ROLE-REG), ex-officio member-secretary;
3.3 Academic Deans: Dean of Academic Affairs, Dean of Research, Dean of Student Welfare;
3.4 Chief Finance Officer (ROLE-CFO): Financial advisor;
3.5 External Nominees: Eminent educationalists, industrial leaders, and state government representatives.

4. POWERS, AUTHORITIES, AND FUNCTIONS
The Executive Council possesses statutory powers to:
- Ratify the annual fiscal budget, fee schedules (FIN-POL-001), and audited balance sheets;
- Create academic departments, research centers, and professorial chairs;
- Approve all major campus infrastructure investments and estate expansions;
- Serve as the final appellate authority for severe employee and student disciplinary expulsions.

1. PURPOSE AND GLOBAL MOBILITY SCOPE
This policy establishes the eligibility benchmarks, application procedures, credit equivalency formulas, and institutional support mechanisms governing outbound student semester exchange and dual-degree programs operated with accredited foreign partner institutions. Administered by the Office of International Relations (DEPT-IRO).

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY AND ENTITY DISAMBIGUATION (IR)
Within Demo University administrative documentation, the abbreviation 'IR' represents three distinct entities:
2.1 Office of International Relations (IR under DEPT-IRO / IRO-POL-001): The administrative division overseeing study abroad mobility, international dual degrees, and foreign memoranda of understanding;
2.2 Institutional Repository (IR under DEPT-LIB / LIB-POL-004): The digital research library archive holding dissertations, theses, and publications;
2.3 Incident Report (IR under DEPT-SEC / SEC-SOP-003): The formal security documentation protocol logging campus thefts or safety incidents.
- Disambiguation Rule: Text referencing foreign universities, visas, exchange semesters, or overseas partner institutions maps strictly to International Relations (IRO-POL-001).

3. ELIGIBILITY AND APPLICATION WORKFLOW
3.1 Eligibility: Open to undergraduate students who have completed four semesters with a CGPA >= 7.50 and zero active backlogs;
3.2 Application: Students submit applications through UniCampus ERP attaching statements of purpose and language proficiency scores;
3.3 Credit Transfer: Pre-approved course syllabi transfer directly into Demo University academic records upon official transcript receipt.
